    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the confusion. Here's a more clinical approach to your heifer's recovery post-rumenotomy:\n\n1. Infection Symptoms:\n   - Elevated body temperature: Monitor for a fever, which is typically above 39.2°C (102°F).\n   - Purulent discharge: Look for yellow or greenish pus from the surgical site, indicating a bacterial infection.\n   - Swelling or redness: Any significant swelling, redness, or heat around the incision site.\n   - Lameness or reluctance to move: If the heifer is showing signs of discomfort or avoiding movement, this could be a sign of infection.\n   - Changes in appetite or behavior: A decrease in appetite or unusual behavior could indicate an underlying issue.\n\n2. Feed Transition Timeline:\n   - Day 1-2: Offer small amounts of easily digestible feed, such as soaked hay or a specialized heifer starter.\n   - Day 3-4: Gradually increase the quantity and complexity of the feed, while still monitoring for any signs of discomfort or illness.\n   - Day 5-7: If the heifer is showing no signs of infection and appears to be recovering well, you can start introducing her to her regular diet, but continue to monitor",
      "source": "target",
